      "knowledge_point": "Solving a Quadratic Equation When the Solutions Are Irrational",
      "problem": "The equation ${m}^{2}+8=-8m$ can be written in the form $(m+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, the $-8m$ term to the left-hand side, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+8 & =-8m \\\\ {m}^{2}+8m & =-8 \\\\ {m}^{2}+2\\cdot 4\\cdot m & =-8 \\\\ {m}^{2}+2\\cdot 4\\cdot m+{4}^{2}-{4}^{2} & =-8 \\\\ (m+4)^{2}-{4}^{2} & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(m+4)^{2}-{4}^{2} & =-8 \\\\ (m+4)^{2} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=4,q=8,$ and $p+q=12.$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(m+4)^{2} & =8 \\\\ \\sqrt{(m+4)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\ |m+4| & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\ m+4 & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ m & =-4\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
